+
+## Round 41 — module contract completion (implemented)
+
+### 1. Cyclic imports: `E_MODULE_CYCLE`
+
+A read-only pre-pass (`check_module_graph` in `src/sutram_compiler.asm`) runs a
+three-colour DFS over the import graph before textual expansion. Grey
+("visiting") → grey is a back edge and aborts compilation with:
+
+```
+<file>:<line>: Sutram Error [E_MODULE_CYCLE]: cyclic import: a -> b -> a
+```
+
+`<file>:<line>` is the location of the `ayojan` directive that closes the
+cycle; the path lists the actual cycle (trimmed to start at its first
+occurrence), not the full import chain. The pass uses the same three-step
+module resolution as expansion (source dir, executable dir, CWD) so the graph
+it checks is the graph that will be expanded. Missing files are skipped here
+and reported by the expansion phase as before. Diamond imports (already
+"black") are unaffected. Regression: `examples/210_r41_cycle.sm`.
+
+### 2. Export visibility: `niryat`
+
+Opt-in via `# sutram-module-v1` in **both** the importing file and the module.
+A v1 module declares exports with `niryat <name>` (one per line, top level):
+
+- Duplicate `niryat` of the same name is
+  `E_MODULE_DUP_EXPORT` with `file:line` (`examples/213_r41_v1_dupexp.sm`).
+- A v1 module **must** be imported with an alias (`ayojan mod@alias`);
+  otherwise `E_MODULE_V1_ALIAS` (`examples/214_r41_v1_noalias.sm`).
+- Only exported names are publicly visible as `alias__name`. A reference to
+  `alias__<private>` from any file other than the defining module's own is
+  `E_MODULE_NOT_EXPORTED` with `file:line` (`examples/212_r41_v1_private.sm`).
+  The check skips `#`/`//` comments and `"..."` strings and requires the
+  reference to be a call (`name` followed by optional space/tab and `(`),
+  matching the renamer's behaviour.
+- `niryat` lines are stripped from the module text before the namespaced
+  rewrite, so they never reach the parser. Legacy (non-v1) imports are
+  byte-for-byte unchanged.
+
+Deliberate scoping: visibility is enforced for the aliased (`@alias`) import
+form, which is the documented v1 contract. Unqualified imports of v1 modules
+are rejected (see `E_MODULE_V1_ALIAS`); unqualified legacy imports keep their
+exact historical shared-namespace behaviour.
+
+### 3. Module initialisation: defined semantics
+
+Sutram modules (`.smlib`) contain only `prakriya` definitions — there is no
+top-level executable code, so "initialisation" is the order in which module
+sources are inlined into the single translation unit. The defined, implemented
+semantics are:
+
+1. **Once-only.** Each module's source is inlined exactly once per compilation
+   (the `import_names` visited table). A diamond (`a -> b,c; b -> d; c -> d`)
+   inlines `d` once; verified by byte-identical output against a manually
+   flattened equivalent (R40).
+2. **Deterministic order.** Expansion is multi-pass and breadth-first over the
+   import graph: pass 1 inlines the root's `ayojan` directives left-to-right;
+   each subsequent pass inlines directives introduced by the previous pass, in
+   the order they appear in the expanded text; already-visited modules are
+   skipped. The root's own code is the base text and is therefore last. The
+   same inputs always produce byte-identical output (verified: repeated
+   compilations of diamond and module examples are byte-identical).
+3. **No forward-reference hazard.** Function definitions are order-independent:
+   calls are patched after code generation (`bind_calls`), so inlining order
+   has no observable effect on program behaviour — only on definition order in
+   the object, which is deterministic per (2).
+
+What is still NOT implemented (unchanged from R40): object-file separate
+compilation (`.smo`), module-cache reuse across translation units, and
+Devanagari `niryat` (ASCII `niryat` only).
